g  um for example granzyme  or interfering gamma which are key for  mediating t cell effector forms  then instead of actually providing the  antigen to the co  culture we instead treated the tumor  cells with epigenetic modulator  modulator 102 to see if interfering with  several of these pathways we could  achieve the same effect so  induce antigen expression  in a tumor that normally does not  express this antigen  and as you can see for the epigenetic  modulator number one  we actually do get a good response it  seems like the  tumor cell is now after treatment able  to be recognized  um by the t cell and from other data we  know that it does indeed  now express um the antigen and present  it on its mhc molecule  epigenetic modulator number two on the  other hand  didn't really work that well  we wanted to make sure that this  observed behavior was indeed due to the  tumor cell antigen presentation pathway  which needs a whole machinery from gene  expression to protein production  and processing of those proteins to the  eventual loading of the antigenic  fragment  onto the cellular mhc models before a t  cell receptor can recognize the tumor  cell  therefore for this project we created  some crispr  knockouts of key proteins in those  pathways  and those are the other two cell lines  you see across the bottom in purple and  in blue  we made a beta 2 mko the better 2 mko is  an essential component of the mhc  complex on the salt surface  so in the absence of this protein no  functional mhc  complex can be formed and the cell is  not present it is not capable of  presenting any antigens whether they  come from outside or inside of the cell  the second is the temp1 ko so tap1 is an  essential transporter  for intracellular antigen processing and  antigen loading onto the mhc  so in the absence of this protein those  particular cells  are not able to present newly expressed  near antigens but are expected to have a  functional mhc  on the surface and indeed our cytokine  data  using the antigen loaded control confirm  what we expected the tumor cells without  a functional mhc at all the beta2mko  no longer induces t cell activations  even when we load the antigen externally  however the type 1ko still has an mhc  and is therefore able to perform like  the wild type  in both case our epigenetic modulators  don't work anymore to the deficiencies  in the antigen presentation pathway  which together with other data gave us  confidence that the  approach is promising so this is an  example where we use complex cellular  models and routine profiling of t-cell  activation markers  to better understand the mechanisms and  validate targets  in io  my next case studies comes more from the  mouse in vivo work that we do  specifically from an osotopic transplant  model of pancreatic cancer  here the specific question was if  radiation therapy  a standard of care approach can enhance  immunotherapy  efficacy of pd-1 inhibitors or  combinations of pd1 and ido inhibitors  ultimately the data revealed that  survival was improved by a combination  of radiation and anti-pd-1  but that ideo combinations were not  useful  but that the efficacy of the p1 activity  correlated with the systemic  interference gamma response  that we monitored by multiplex  phytochrome profiling  as my third example i want to give you a  flavor for how we use  phytochrome profiling to evaluate the  toxicity risks of our later stage  molecules before filing an ind  in this particular example a bms  molecule was given in increasing doses  group 1 is the control group and group 2  and 4  2 through 4 received the drug twice a  week apart and we received serum samples  for cytokine profiling at different time  points  from just before the first dose until  about two weeks after the last dose  here we assess the total of 44 different  cytokines in  two different thinner specific panels  i have just chosen five relevant ones  for the line graph and  each line in the graph represents an  individual monkey  we observed a dose-dependent transient  increase in inflammatory cytokines that  peaks three to six hours after dosing  and returns to baseline within 24 hours  we also saw that the second dose doesn't  cause the same magnitude of effect  together with other data that was  collected from the  study we could conclude that the  molecule was well tolerated  and did not cause a cytokine storm which  was a particular concern  for this particular mechanism of action  of this molecule  because we could also from in vitro data  we had previously generated figure out  that these transient cytokine inductions  that we see  are most likely on mechanism and on  target  in my last example i want to talk to you  very briefly about our work  with patient samples and this is where  we most broadly apply the checkpoint  panel one that jan  had introduced when we obtain these  types of patient samples we typically  perform very very broad profiling  as much typically as sample volumes  permit  often for a hundred or more analytes  including the checkpoint panel  um so the heat map exemplifies  an example of what such data could look  like  those aggregate several hundred serum  samples by different tumor types  which shows clear differences in the  checkpoint molecules detected  ultimately the goal is to build a  sufficiently large database that allows  us to identify  predictive biomarker signatures when  linked to patient phenotypic  or treatment data and another focus for  us is also  performing longitudinal studies on  treatment  particularly with ntp1 to investigate  the response to treatment or the  development of resistance mechanism  and their impact on the respective  biomarker signatures  further what we also frequently do is  use  explants to evaluate individual  treatments and treatment combos  and with biomarker data the multiplex  luminex data is typically one key  readout of those  types of studies  with that i was i hope i was able to  give you a flavor for how we use  multiplex biomarker profiling  in io discovery at bmf and i want to  finish with an  outlook on our current and future focus  areas  an ever ongoing effort is the expansion  of coverage  so adding new panels of analytes to the  platform  or developing new analytes all together  in close collaboration with millipore  we also have a growing need for custom  disease-specific panels  to support targeted biomarker studies  and we also are always very interested  in trying to  consolidate and merge individual panels  wherever possible  to both reduce workloads and the volumes  of sample that are needed  finally we also spend a lot of effort in  two  closely related areas data analytics and  data integration  i have shown you that we profile tens of  thousands of samples a year across a  wide variety of experimental design  with that comes a strong need for data  management  and more importantly meta data  management so all the data can be  applied for the right as possible use so  we are constantly trying to optimize  our data registration and database  models or build new systems to handle  these types of data  this is also key in facilitating  enhanced bioinformatics methods for data  analysis  as well as efforts to implement machine  learning  particularly for generating disease  predictive biomarker signatures  finally biomarker data becomes much more  informative when it can be overlaid with  other data types generated from the same  types of studies  such as sequencing of phenotyping data  for example  so we are trying to build structures  that enable to do that more easily  and lastly an effort that we have  ongoing  focuses on new technologies that allows  investigating the cytokine profile of a  single cell  as we know immune cells in particular  show very heterogeneous responses to  treatment  and ultimately we want to marry that  single cell data to the bulk biomarker  data that we routinely produce from the  luminex profiling  with that i want to thank you for your  attention  and acknowledge and particularly the bms  cytokine team  especially melissa yard and nathan  cheadle who are the engines of this  platform and keep the sample and data  flowing on a day-by-day basis  and we are also indebted to all of our  bms collaborators both  those who enable us to physically run  all the instruments that we need  as well as the partners in the  individual therapeutic  areas who supply us with the samples and  i also want to acknowledge our very  strong partners  at miliport particularly jan and shane  who we meet with frequently and  non-pandemic times  as well as brook who supports our ever  expanding list of requests  for new analytes and with that um  thank you very much again  and i'll hand it back over to andy  all right thank you jenny thank you  jenny and jan for this great  presentation
 

